---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/LegalLayout.astro";

const title = "Help & Guide | PaintingPricing Calculator";
const description = "A complete walkthrough of every part of the Pro workspace, with screenshots, and how each one helps you price and run real jobs.";

const SECTIONS = [
  { id: "overview", label: "Overview" },
  { id: "business-settings", label: "Business settings" },
  { id: "paint-catalog", label: "Paint & Materials" },
  { id: "projects", label: "Projects" },
  { id: "customer-document", label: "Customer document" },
  { id: "price-book-health", label: "Price Book Health" },
  { id: "actual-costs", label: "Actual Costs" },
  { id: "revisions", label: "Revisions" },
  { id: "backup", label: "Backup & Data" },
  { id: "free-tools", label: "Free tools" },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  eyebrow="Help"
  heading="Help & guide"
  updated="September 19, 2026"
>
  <section>
    <p>
      This is a complete walkthrough of the Pro workspace — every tab, what
      it does, and why it's there. New to Pro? See{" "}
      <a href="/pricing">what's included and the current price</a> first, or
      the <a href="/app/welcome">Welcome page</a> for a faster five-step
      version of just the "how do I start" part. This page goes deeper,
      section by section, with real screenshots.
    </p>
    <nav aria-label="Sections" class="mt-4 flex flex-wrap gap-x-4 gap-y-1 text-sm">
      {
        SECTIONS.map((s) => (
          <a href={`#${s.id}`} class="text-link">
            {s.label}
          </a>
        ))
      }
    </nav>
  </section>

  <section id="overview">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      The dashboard you land on when you open Pro. Four at-a-glance numbers —
      active projects, estimates issued this month, estimated revenue, and
      your average gross margin — followed by your most recently updated
      projects, a Quick actions panel, and a Price Book Health summary with
      how many of your priced services are at or above your target margin.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Why it helps you:</strong> it's a snapshot of how your
      estimating business is actually doing, not just a place to start a new
      quote — you see at a glance whether recent jobs cleared your target
      margin before you dig into any one project. A brand-new, empty
      workspace shows a five-step "how to get started" guide here instead,
      so the very first screen you see tells you exactly what to do next.
    </p>
    <img
      src="/guide/overview.png"
      alt="The Overview dashboard with one active project, one estimate this month, estimated revenue, average gross margin, a recent-projects table, Quick actions, and a Price Book Health summary"
      width="1200"
      height="1144"
      loading="lazy"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="business-settings">
    <h2>Business settings</h2>
    <p>
      This is where you set the numbers every estimate is built from: your
      loaded hourly rate, overhead ratio, target margin, and how fast you
      cover a wall, ceiling, trim run, or door (per coat). Sensible defaults
      are pre-filled so you can start immediately — but the sooner you set
      these to your real numbers, the less re-work later.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Why it helps you:</strong> every project starts from a
      snapshot of these settings, so you only enter your rates once instead
      of re-typing them into every estimate. Changing a setting here never
      silently rewrites an estimate you've already saved — a project only
      picks up new numbers when you explicitly run a rate refresh on it, so
      an old quote can't drift out from under you.
    </p>
    <img
      src="/guide/business-settings.png"
      alt="The Business Settings tab, showing loaded hourly rate, overhead, target margin, and per-surface throughput fields"
      width="1200"
      height="1144"
      loading="lazy"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="paint-catalog">
    <h2>Paint & Materials</h2>
    <p>
      Your list of paint products — name, price per gallon, coverage, and
      sheen. Every room, surface, and Price Book Health service picks its
      paint from this list, so it's the one thing you need before you can
      build an estimate or add a service.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Why it helps you:</strong> enter a product's real price once
      and every estimate that uses it stays accurate — update a price here
      when your supplier's prices change, rather than hunting down every
      estimate that used it.
    </p>
    <img
      src="/guide/paint-catalog.png"
      alt="The Paint & Materials tab with three paint products, each with name, price per gallon, coverage, and sheen"
      width="1200"
      height="1144"
      loading="lazy"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="projects">
    <h2>Projects</h2>
    <p>
      This is the actual estimate-building workspace — one entry per real
      job. Click <strong>"+ New project"</strong> and fill in your business
      info and your customer's info — this is exactly what appears on the
      customer-facing document later, so it's worth getting right up front.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then add each room — length, width, height, and how many doors/windows
      to deduct (a quick estimate, or exact measured openings). Each room
      automatically gets a wall surface, with an optional ceiling. Need a
      standalone trim run or door outside any room? Add those separately,
      right below the room list.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Why it helps you:</strong> the geometry math (gross wall area,
      minus openings, times coats, divided by coverage, plus waste) is
      exactly the kind of arithmetic that's easy to get wrong by hand on a
      real job with several rooms — here it's automatic and consistent
      every time.
    </p>
    <p>
      As you add rooms and surfaces, the <strong>Estimate summary</strong>{" "}
      fills in live: materials, labor, direct cost, overhead, and your
      estimated job cost, followed by a suggested price at your target
      margin (or your own custom price) with profit, margin, and a
      pricing-status badge.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Why it helps you:</strong> you see your real margin on this
      specific job before you send the quote — not after the job is done
      and it's too late to change the price. "Save draft" works at any
      point, even on an incomplete estimate; "Issue estimate" is only
      enabled once the calculation is complete, so you can't accidentally
      send a customer an estimate built on missing information.
    </p>
    <p>Once you've saved a project, it shows up back in your Projects list, and in the Overview dashboard's recent-projects table.</p>
    <img
      src="/guide/project-info.png"
      alt="The Business & customer info card, filled in with a business name, contact, address, and the customer's name, contact, and job-site address"
      width="846"
      height="294"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <img
      src="/guide/project-rooms.png"
      alt="A room editor showing a 16 by 13 by 9 foot Living Room with opening deduction enabled for 1 door and 2 windows, and its auto-created Wall surface"
      width="846"
      height="639"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <img
      src="/guide/estimate-summary.png"
      alt="The Estimate summary card showing Materials, Labor, Direct cost, Overhead, Estimated job cost, and a Proposed price with Profit, Margin, and an 'Above target' status badge"
      width="846"
      height="486"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <img
      src="/guide/projects-list.png"
      alt="The Projects list showing one saved project, '123 Oak Street — Exterior Repaint', marked issued, revision 1"
      width="1200"
      height="1144"
      loading="lazy"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="customer-document">
    <h2>Customer document</h2>
    <p>
      Once you issue an estimate, a clean customer-facing document appears
      below it — ready to print or save as a PDF: your business info, the
      project title and address, your customer's name, an estimate number
      and date, a line item, and a total price.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Why it helps you:</strong> this view never shows your cost,
      overhead, labor rate, or margin — only what the customer needs to see
      (the job, the price, your business info). You never have to remember
      to hide your numbers or build a separate quote template by hand.
    </p>
    <img
      src="/guide/customer-document.png"
      alt="The customer-facing document: business info, project title and address, customer name, estimate number and date, a line item, and a total price -- with no cost, overhead, or margin figures anywhere on it"
      width="846"
      height="369"
      loading="lazy"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="price-book-health">
    <h2>Price Book Health</h2>
    <p>
      A separate concept from project estimating: your own standing list of
      named services (like "interior wall painting" or "standard door,
      2 sides") each priced per unit — per square foot, per linear foot, or
      per door — independent of any specific job.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Why it helps you:</strong> this is where you sanity-check your
      standing menu prices, not a one-off job quote. If you've been
      charging the same per-square-foot rate for a while, Price Book Health
      shows whether that rate still covers your real modeled cost and target
      margin — catching a price that's quietly gone underwater as your
      material or labor costs changed, before it costs you money on the
      next ten jobs that use it.
    </p>
    <img
      src="/guide/price-book-health.png"
      alt="A Price Book Health service card for 'Interior wall painting' showing modeled cost per unit, current price, status 'Above target', margin, and required price for target margin"
      width="1200"
      height="1144"
      loading="lazy"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="actual-costs">
    <h2>Actual Costs</h2>
    <p>
      Once you've issued an estimate and finished the job, open that project
      and come back here to record what it actually cost — materials, labor
      (entered directly or as hours × rate), other expenses, and overhead
      (either the amount already allocated in the estimate, or a real
      measured figure).
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Why it helps you:</strong> this closes the loop between what
      you quoted and what the job really cost — profit vs. your original
      quote, your real margin, and the total variance, all in one place.
      Over time, this is how you learn whether your rates and throughput
      assumptions actually hold up on real jobs, not just on paper.
    </p>
    <img
      src="/guide/actual-review.png"
      alt="The Actual Costs tab with Materials, Labor, Other Expenses, and Overhead all confirmed, showing Actual cost, Profit vs. original quote, Margin, and Total variance vs. estimate"
      width="1200"
      height="1144"
      loading="lazy"
    />
  </section>

  <section id="revisions">
    <h2>Revisions</h2>
    <p>
      Every estimate you issue becomes a frozen revision — its numbers are
      locked in exactly as sent, even if you later change your business
      settings or catalog prices. Need to change something after issuing?
      Click "Edit," which creates a new draft revision without touching the
      one you already sent. Issuing that new draft automatically supersedes
      the old one, so there's always exactly one active revision per
      project. If a project has more than one revision, a row of buttons
      lets you look back at any earlier one — viewing an old revision never
      changes which one is currently active.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Why it helps you:</strong> you can always answer "what did I
      actually quote this customer, and when" — even after your rates have
      changed since then — without worrying that reviewing an old quote
      might accidentally reactivate it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="backup">
    <h2>Backup &amp; Data</h2>
    <p>
      Everything in Pro — every project, revision, actual-cost review, your
      catalog, your services, your settings — lives only in this browser.
      The Backup &amp; Data tab is how you protect that.
    </p>
    <img
      src="/guide/backup.png"
      alt="The Backup & Data tab with an 'Export backup (.json)' button and a 'Restore from backup' section with an import-mode dropdown and file picker"
      width="1200"
      height="1144"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <p><strong>Export backup (.json)</strong> downloads everything in one file. Keep it somewhere safe — it's the only way to recover your data if you ever clear your browser's storage or switch devices.</p>
    <p>Restoring from a backup file gives you three modes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Merge with existing (default)</strong> — previews exactly
        what would change (new projects, variants, services to add;
        identical records to skip) and lets you choose, per conflict, to
        keep your local version, use the imported one, or keep both.
        Nothing is written until you confirm.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Import as copies</strong> — brings in every project from
        the file as a fresh copy, so you can pull data from an old backup
        without disturbing what's already here.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Replace everything</strong> — a full wipe-and-restore from
        the file, for starting over from a known-good backup. It forces
        you to download a backup of your current data first, so this is
        never a one-click accident.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      <strong>Why it helps you:</strong> your data has nowhere else to live
      — there's no server copy — so a regular export is the difference
      between "I switched laptops" being a non-event and being a real
      loss. The three import modes mean moving data between devices,
      recovering from a mistake, or starting fresh are all deliberate,
      previewed actions, never a silent overwrite.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Ready to get started?</h2>
    <p>
      See the current <a href="/pricing">Pro pricing</a> — one-time
      payment, lifetime access, and the guarantee — or jump straight into{" "}
      <a href="/app">the app</a> if you're already unlocking it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="free-tools">
    <h2>Not ready for Pro yet?</h2>
    <p>Three free tools cover a lot of ground before you need the full workspace:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/free/job-cost-calculator">Painting job cost calculator</a> —
        materials, labor, and overhead in, an implied price out.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/free/interior-calculator">Interior painting cost calculator</a> —
        paint and labor for a single room.
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/free/estimate-template">Painting estimate template</a> —
        a printable line-item estimate, no account needed.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Still have a question?</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="/contact">Get in touch</a> — we're happy to help.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
